**Read:** [Nonvolatile residue collection and analysis — Gordon, Rutherford and Aranda](../sources/Nonvolatile-Residue-Collection-and-Analysis-NASA.lingua#r_2YMCRSWR07NCHECM0X4D8FJ6HJ), sampling boundaries; [A specimen, a sampling target, and a result about a population](../../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH) and [Sampling contributions, thresholds, and results near zero](../../../inquiry/metrology/uncertainty/Sampling-and-censoring.lingua#r_3RRJRMNNAPMVYJ9Z6VGY2DV0CG), target and sampling design.

A convenient micrograph is evidence about its observed field. Estimating a whole-surface mean requires a sampling design tied to that surface. Targeted inspection of likely problem sites and random sampling answer different questions and can be used together when their roles stay explicit.

In a simple independent model, suppose a small observation lands in a contaminated region with probability f and detects it perfectly. The probability that n observations all miss it is (1 − f)ⁿ. With f = 0.05 and n = 20, this is about 0.358486. For miss probability at most 0.05, n must be at least 59 under these assumptions. Spatial correlation, finite field area and imperfect detection change the model.

**Work:** Derive the result and compare random points, a regular grid and targeted observations against a hypothetical striped contamination pattern. Identify a grid that repeatedly misses the pattern. Keep a map of observed areas and distinguish observed absence from an inference about unobserved areas. Relate the sampling effort to the functional consequence of a localized deposit.

